# Proposed by the swarm's review (2026-10-14), from the chaos harness's `vanished` rule; the documenter may edit or drop it.
# Story: five windows arranged, so the Desktop is a tile, and a note floating over them. Out of habit the user presses the
# shortcut for home, though home is what is showing. Nothing a person can see may change: the note stays.
# Only when the view really moves does the note go with the Desktop, and it comes back with it.
# Reference: T03 T52.
open A
open B
open C
open D
open E
arrange
open Note 500x400
expect visible 106
home
expect visible 106
show-all
expect visible 106
zoom 101
expect hidden 106
desktop
expect visible 106
# The Desktop is a tile in the widest view, and still part of it: after "show everything" nothing is out of view.
zoom 101
expect hidden 106
home
expect visible 106
# Where it was, to the point (Q20).
expect at 106 300 200 500x400
# Until 2026-10-15 this ended `expect in-front 106`, which took a raise. It is gone on purpose; putting it back is no fix. Arbor raises nothing but
# a window kept in view, and going out of view and coming back changes no window's place in macOS's order (QUESTIONS Q20, Q25). Why: it is what
# the Mac would do without Arbor. A window that gets the keyboard comes over whatever floated above it, so the note lies under the window somebody
# has just worked in and over the other four as before: the order is what their own acts made it. The same raise put a guest over the
# window its person had just switched to. "In view" is a place, not a promise that nothing covers it.
# ⌃⌥D, or a click on the Desktop's tile, shows all that floats.
desktop
expect visible 106
expect in-front 106
